/**

\page tarch_pytest Pytest-based tests for Peano's Python scripts

\tableofcontents

All tests in [tests/](../../tests) are driven by `pytest`. They are kept
_separate_ from Peano's C++ unit tests in [src/unittests/](../../src/unittests),
which `make check` or `ctest` execute (see \ref tarch_tests). The pytest
workflow does not replace these C++ tests. It covers the Python layer that
constructs Peano applications, and it runs whole generated experiments.


Kinds of tests
--------------

We distinguish three kinds of tests via pytest markers:

- **Unit tests** have no marker. They are fast, test importable Python
  logic, never compile anything, and run by default.
- **Integration tests** carry `@pytest.mark.integration`. They are slow or
  need a configured and built Peano, as they typically generate a project,
  compile it and run the executable.
- **Slow tests** carry `@pytest.mark.slow`, either alone or in combination
  with `integration`. Examples are physics test cases which run for minutes.

Both markers are registered in [pytest.ini](../../pytest.ini). Plain pytest
runs skip all tests that carry either marker.


Folder layout
-------------

- `tests/unit/` holds the fast unit tests of the Python API.
- `tests/integration/` holds integration tests of the Python API which do not
  compile code, such as the load balancing tests. Shared helpers live in
  plain Python modules next to the tests, e.g.
  [tree_builders.py](../../tests/integration/tree_builders.py).
- `tests/exaparty/` holds the ExaParty test cases. Every case directory has a
  run script which generates, compiles and runs the experiment.
  [tests/exaparty/test_exaparty.py](../../tests/exaparty/test_exaparty.py)
  invokes these run scripts one by one, i.e. each run script becomes one pytest
  test. A test passes if its run script returns exit code zero. See
  \ref exaparty_tests.

Some ExaParty generator scripts are named `test_*.py` but are command-line
scripts rather than pytest modules. [tests/conftest.py](../../tests/conftest.py)
excludes them from collection. Do not use the `test_` prefix for new scripts
which are not pytest modules.

The test cases in `tests/exahype2/` and `tests/multigrid/` still use the legacy
Python API in `python/` and the MGHyPE API, respectively. They are not driven
by pytest yet. Run their scripts manually.


Pytest configuration
--------------------

[pytest.ini](../../pytest.ini) configures pytest centrally:

- pytest searches all of `tests/` for files matching `test_*.py`.
- `src/` is added to the Python path, so the tests find `peano4.api`,
  `exaparty.api`, `toolbox`, ... without an editable install.
- `addopts` deselects all tests marked as `integration` or `slow`. A `-m`
  argument on the command line overrides this default.

[tests/conftest.py](../../tests/conftest.py) furthermore redirects the log
file of `peano4.api.Logger` into a temporary directory, so test runs do not
leave `log-<timestamp>.md` files in the repository.


Local execution
---------------

pytest is part of [requirements.txt](../../requirements.txt), i.e. it is
installed alongside all other Python dependencies (see \ref page_installation_home).
Run all commands from Peano's root directory.

Run the unit tests:

\code{.sh}
python3 -m pytest
\endcode

Run the integration tests of the Python API:

\code{.sh}
python3 -m pytest -m integration tests/integration
\endcode

Run the ExaParty test cases. They need a Peano build with
`--enable-particles --enable-exaparty` and are skipped otherwise:

\code{.sh}
python3 -m pytest -m "integration and not slow" tests/exaparty   # functional tests only
python3 -m pytest -m integration tests/exaparty                  # including physics tests
python3 -m pytest -m integration tests/exaparty -k sod-1d        # one test case
\endcode

The ExaParty tests build against the Peano installation which the generated
Makefiles use: `PEANO_SRC_ROOT_DIR` and `PEANO_CMAKE_BUILD_DIR` are honoured
if set, otherwise the tests use the repository they belong to. Add `-s` to
see the output of the generator scripts and executables while they run.


Continuous integration
----------------------

The continuous integration runs pytest, too (see \ref page_continuous_integration):

- The job `Python_Unit_Tests` runs `python3 -m pytest`.
- The ExaParty jobs run `python3 -m pytest -m integration tests/exaparty -k <case>`.

Both are defined in `.continuous_integration/templates/python-tests.yml` and
`.continuous_integration/templates/exaparty.yml`.


Writing new tests
-----------------

- Put fast tests of Python logic into `tests/unit/test_<topic>.py`. Test
  argument parsing, small helpers, configuration builders and pure-Python
  transformations such as template or string generation.
- Mark everything that generates and compiles code, needs MPI, PETSc or
  other third-party libraries, or takes more than a few seconds with
  `@pytest.mark.integration` and, if it takes minutes, also with
  `@pytest.mark.slow`.
- For a new ExaParty test case, add a run script that returns a non-zero exit
  code if the test fails, i.e. use `set -e` and end with a comparison script
  which calls `sys.exit` with the number of errors. Then add the run script
  to `FUNCTIONAL_TESTS` or `PHYSICS_TESTS` in
  [tests/exaparty/test_exaparty.py](../../tests/exaparty/test_exaparty.py).
- Pytest test files need unique base names across `tests/`, as pytest imports
  them as top-level modules.

*/
